[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 703,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 466,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105510,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 482,
        "headingId": 466,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Hyaline fibromatosis syndrome (HFS) is a rare autosomal recessive disease characterized by abnormal deposits of amorphous substance in the dermis, subcutaneous soft tissues, gingiva, and visceral sites, with clinical manifestations varying from very severe (infantile systemic hyalinosis) to milder (juvenile hyaline fibromatosis).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:31:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 704,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 467,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105511,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 482,
        "headingId": 467,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:31:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 705,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 468,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105512,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 482,
        "headingId": 468,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>EE6Y Other specified fibromatous disorders of skin and soft tissue</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:31:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 706,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 469,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105513,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 482,
        "headingId": 469,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> infantile systemic hyalinosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:31:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 707,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 470,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105514,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 482,
        "headingId": 470,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:31:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 708,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 471,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105515,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 482,
        "headingId": 471,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>HFS is a multisystem disease and involves the skin, subcutaneous tissue, viscera, and bone.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:31:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 709,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 472,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105516,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 482,
        "headingId": 472,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>HFS manifests in infancy or later in childhood as slow-growing papular and nodular skin lesions {17284973; 30176098; 22383261}. Patients affected by juvenile hyaline fibromatosis show joint contractures (in 85&ndash;95% of cases), gingival hyperplasia, osteopenia, and osteolytic lesions. Infantile systemic hyalinosis frequently manifests with extreme pain at minimal handling. Visceral involvement with intractable diarrhoea (in 50%) and recurrent infections (in 30%) lead to failure to thrive and death {30176098; 22383261; 19344977}. A grading system for HFS has been proposed: grade&nbsp;1,&nbsp;mild (skin and/or gingiva); grade&nbsp;2, moderate (joint and/or bone); grade&nbsp;3, severe (internal organ); and grade&nbsp;4, life-limiting {19344977; 22771664}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:31:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 710,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 473,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105517,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 482,
        "headingId": 473,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>HFS is rare. The incidence is unknown.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:31:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 711,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 474,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105518,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 482,
        "headingId": 474,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>HFS is caused by loss-of-function mutations including inactivating homozygous or compound heterozygous mutations in the <em>ANTXR2</em> (<em>CMG2</em>) gene {29801470; 30176098; 17284973}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:31:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 712,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 475,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105519,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 482,
        "headingId": 475,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>HFS is caused by sequence variants in the <em>ANTXR2</em> gene, which encodes a protein involved in basement membrane matrix assembly (in particular collagen&nbsp;VI homeostasis) and endothelial cell morphogenesis {17284973; 22215446; 28604699}. Phenotype&ndash;genotype correlations have demonstrated that <em>ANTXR2</em> missense mutations in exons&nbsp;1&ndash;12 and mutations leading to a premature stop codon more commonly lead to severe disease, whereas missense mutations in exons&nbsp;13&ndash;17 lead to a mild form of disease {30176098; 29801470; 22215446; 20331448}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:31:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 719,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 482,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105520,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 482,
        "headingId": 482,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The nodules are solid and homogeneous.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:31:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 713,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 476,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105521,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 482,
        "headingId": 476,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>HFS lesions are characterized by plump to spindled uniform fibroblasts, often arranged in cords simulating vascular spaces, admixed with abundant extracellular eosinophilic, non-fibrillar hyaline material, obliterating normal structures {22383261; 15078358}. The hyaline-like extracellular matrix deposits stain strongly with PAS and are diastase-resistant {22383261; 15078358}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:31:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 714,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 477,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105522,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 482,
        "headingId": 477,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:31:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 715,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 478,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105523,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 482,
        "headingId": 478,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Molecular testing is necessary to demonstrate a germline mutation of <em>ANTXR2</em>.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:31:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 718,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 481,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105524,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 482,
        "headingId": 481,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> subcutaneous nodules and gingival hypertrophy with prominent hyaline-like material.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> germline sequencing of <em>ANTXR2</em>.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:31:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 716,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 479,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105525,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 482,
        "headingId": 479,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:31:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 717,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 480,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105526,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 482,
        "headingId": 480,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Earlier presentation is associated with more severe disease, systemic involvement, and poor prognosis (median age at death: 15&nbsp;months) {30176098; 19344977; 22383261}. In milder forms, wheelchair use may become necessary. Treatment is largely supportive. Surgical excision of subcutaneous nodules and/or gingivectomy for gingival hypertrophy is often performed; however, recurrences are common {30176098; 22383261}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:31:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]